// interFoam REFERENCE ARM for the 2D stationary droplet.
//
// WHY. The level-set method's parasitic current converges (its delivered
// non-gradient content at order +2.04, R^2 0.9987) but the coupled run
// destabilises at fine resolution in 3D, while interFoam is BELIEVED to stay
// stable on the same case. Belief is not a measurement: this arm measures it, on
// the same mesh, the same physics, the same pressure boundary treatment and the
// same fixed capillary time step, so the ONLY difference is the interface method.
//
// What interFoam is expected to trade for that stability is convergence: its
// curvature comes from kappa = -div(grad(alpha)/|grad(alpha)|) on the same alpha
// that appears in the force, which is self-consistent (hence well balanced) but
// notoriously non-convergent under refinement. If the measurement shows
// interFoam stable and NOT converging while the level-set method converges and
// destabilises, that is the real comparison and both halves belong in the paper.
values
{
    solver ( interFoam );

    // The 2D ladder of the level-set study, cell for cell.
    N_CELLS ( 64 128 256 512 );

    END_TIME ( 0.1 );

    DOMAIN_LENGTH ( 0.01 );

    // Same capillary step as the level-set cases: MAX_DELTA_T is derived as
    // CAPILLARY_DT_COEFF/nRef^1.5 with nRef = CAPILLARY_REF_LENGTH/h and
    // h = DOMAIN_LENGTH/N_CELLS, so BOTH solvers advance on an identical time grid
    // and any difference in the parasitic current is spatial, not temporal.
    CAPILLARY_DT_COEFF ( 0.010861 );
    CAPILLARY_REF_LENGTH ( 0.01 );
    MAX_CO ( 0.1 );

    // Interface-compression coefficient. 1 is the interFoam default and what every
    // shipped tutorial uses. It is an axis because compression is also a
    // stabiliser: if interFoam's stability on the stationary droplet rests on it,
    // that must be established before any conclusion is drawn about the level-set
    // method, so cAlpha = 0 is the control arm.
    C_ALPHA ( 1 );

    WRITE_INTERVAL ( 0.02 );
}
